: 14.1, "lean": 6.0, "environment": 4.1, "incumbency": 4.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 14.83, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 5.95, "baseline_margin": 14.63, "analyst_adjustment": -0.13, "key_factors": ["Lamont leads by 15-17 points in the latest Quinnipiac and University of New Hampshire polls, with the fall average near 14-15", "D+8 state with a two-term incumbent running for re-election and a favorable environment for Democrats", "All six race raters call it Solid or Safe D, and prediction markets price Lamont around 96-97 percent", "Only the co/efficient poll showed a single-digit race; every other survey since June clusters between 13 and 20 points"], "rationale": "Every recent measurement points the same way: Quinnipiac and UNH put Lamont up 17 in September, the polling average sits near 14-15, and fundamentals in a D+8 state with a popular incumbent add several more points. Markets and raters agree the race is effectively decided, leaving only the size of the margin in doubt.", "watch": "A late shift on cost-of-living and budget issues, the one area where Fazio is competing, could tighten the race, though he would need a far larger move than any poll has shown.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ned Lamont (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Connecticut's governor's race has the shape of a comfortable incumbent re-election. Ned Lamont, seeking a third term, leads Ryan Fazio by double digits in nearly every public poll this fall: Quinnipiac and the University of New Hampshire both put him up 17 points in September, and the polling average sits around 14-15 points. The state's partisan lean, Lamont's incumbency advantage, and a favorable environment all point in the same direction.\n\nFazio, a state senator, has tried to center the race on cost of living and the budget, but Lamont has been on air attacking him since the primary and leads on the economy in polling. Prediction markets put Lamont's chances near 97 percent, and all six major race raters call the contest solid or safe for Democrats. The open question is the size of the margin, not the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 15.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9913, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9893, "margin": 14.5}]}